export default {"":[["What does this page do?","It reads what any website can read about you the moment you open it, then writes up who that makes you: where you're probably from, where you are now, what you use and what you like.\nEach piece of information is a signal: something a site can read without asking you. Hover over a blank in the paragraph to see which signals it came from. The list below shows every signal, how a site reads it (the API or header in the right column) and the value it read. None of them shows a permission prompt."],["How does it guess where I'm from and my city?","Your city comes from your IP address, looked up in Cloudflare's location database, the same kind of database ad networks use. With a VPN on, it's the VPN server's city.\nWhere you're from is a guess based on your browser language (zh-TW points to Taiwan, China; pt-BR to Brazil) and your system's time zone. A VPN changes neither. US English is the default on most devices, so in that case the time zone decides."],["Why can't it tell my age?","Because nothing in the browser says it. Ad networks estimate age and gender from your behavior: which sites you visit, what you watch and what you buy, often linked across sites by the same fingerprint or cookies. This page reads none of that, so it says so instead of making something up."],["What is a browser fingerprint?","Small differences in how your system draws text and shapes (canvas, WebGL) and processes sound, which fonts are installed, plus your screen and settings. Combined into one ID, they come out the same on every site, with no cookies needed, even in private windows.\nIt doesn't depend on your IP, so a VPN doesn't change it. Browsers that protect against it (Brave, Firefox with resistFingerprinting, Safari in private mode) add random noise. The \"Fingerprint protection\" row shows whether yours does."],["How do I use the disguise check?","When you use a VPN or proxy, or change your user agent, sites compare the values they read with each other. These stand out: a system time zone that doesn't match the IP, a language from another country, a WebRTC address that isn't the VPN's, a data center network, a user agent that doesn't match the graphics card.\nTo look like someone in the VPN's country, set your system time zone to match, turn off WebRTC or use a browser that keeps it inside the VPN, and use a residential exit. Different sites seeing different exits doesn't prove a proxy either: a mobile carrier, a company network or security software can split traffic the same way."],["Do you keep any of this?","The checks run in your browser. This site's servers answer three kinds of request only: /api/me (what they see in this very request anyway: your address, Cloudflare's data on it and the headers), /api/etag (a random ID that shows how the browser cache recognizes you, not kept by the server) and the anonymous counts below. The site's own probe, probe.1ms.dog, looks at this connection itself (the TCP packets, the TLS handshake, the start of HTTP/2 and HTTP/3) to tell your system and whether something sits in between. None of them keeps a log.\nTo tell you how common your values are, the page then adds one to anonymous counters: how many checks had this system, browser, screen, graphics card, time zone and first language, the first 8 characters of each fingerprint ID, how each check came out that day, and how long each run took (a range such as 3–5 seconds; a run not done 45 seconds in counts once as stuck). There's no IP, no full fingerprint and no record of a single visit, just one number per value. \"Don't count me\" under the fingerprint card turns this off for this browser. A browser that sends Global Privacy Control is never counted.\nTo find your exits and your DNS resolver, the page also contacts a few outside services directly (named in the right column: IPIP.NET, IP.SB, the Cloudflare trace on chatgpt.com, claude.ai and cloudflare.com, Cloudflare Radar, ip-api's DNS server, and two STUN servers). They see your address, as any site you visit does. Everything else is read in your browser and stays there. The code is open source.\nYour last visit is remembered only in your own browser's storage and never sent anywhere (it's separate from the anonymous counts): the time, fingerprint and city of that visit, so the page can show whether it recognized you. The × on the ID card deletes it. A shared link carries the portrait without your address or city. The \"Stored traces\" row also writes one random tag into localStorage, IndexedDB, Cache Storage and window.name, again only in your browser, to show whether clearing one store is enough; the × deletes those too. Tracker blocking, precise location and motion sensors run only when you press their button: the tracker test asks 7 tracking companies for their scripts, and they see your address; the location and sensor readings are worked out in the page, never sent."],["Why is the graphics card not the one in my computer?","A site can't see the parts in your computer: it reads one string only, the graphics card your browser says it draws this page with.\nLaptops usually have two cards. To save power the browser draws with the integrated one by default, even when there is a discrete card too; with hardware acceleration off (a remote desktop or virtual machine, say) it draws in software and names no card.\nExtensions and anti-detect browsers can report whatever card they like; the disguise check sees that, and the note under the value says it may not be true.\nOn Windows: Settings → System → Display → Graphics, add the browser, set it to High performance, test again. Macs usually have one card."],["Why does the monitor count show more than the one on my desk?","A site counts every display the browser reports, not the ones you can touch. Three things add virtual ones: a virtual-display driver (software such as Duet Display, SuperDisplay or iDisplay turns a tablet into a second screen), a remote-desktop session, or a streaming app (Parsec, Moonlight, TeamViewer) that presents an extra screen. An extended display counts too: a laptop with an external monitor reports two.\nTo bring it down, close the streaming or remote session and quit the virtual-display software, or turn the extra display off in the system's display settings (Windows: Settings → System → Display; macOS: System Settings → Displays). The page can't tell a physical screen from a virtual one, so it just counts what it is told."],["Why do some rows say \"this browser doesn't say\"?","Not every browser offers every API. Keyboard layout, battery, connection type, memory and client hints are Chromium only; Safari and Firefox share less on purpose. A missing value is good for your privacy."]],"fingerprint":[["What is a browser fingerprint?","A set of small differences between computers: how the graphics card and driver draw a canvas or a WebGL scene, how the audio system rounds a sound, which fonts are installed, plus your screen, language and time zone. Each one alone is common; together they're often unique. A site combines them into one ID, and every site that runs the same script gets the same ID for you."],["Does a VPN or a private window change it?","No. None of it depends on your IP address, and private windows draw and play sound the same way. That's why a fingerprint can follow you after you clear cookies or switch VPN servers. The ID card on this page remembers your last visit (in your browser only) so you can see it happen."],["How do I stop being fingerprinted?","Use a browser that fights it: Brave adds random noise to canvas, WebGL and audio by default, Firefox has privacy.resistFingerprinting, and Tor Browser makes every user look the same. Extensions that change the user agent or block canvas often make you stand out more, not less. The \"Fingerprint protection\" row shows whether your browser scrambles it."],["Can this test tell how unique I am?","Roughly, yes. The page counts, anonymously, how many checks had each of your values and your fingerprint ID (its first 8 characters), and shows how many of all the checks on this site matched yours. It keeps no IP and no full fingerprint. The numbers only mean something once enough people have taken the test; until then, the page says the count is still growing."]],"dns-leak":[["What is a DNS leak?","Before your browser connects to a site, a DNS resolver turns the site's name into an address. If that lookup skips the VPN or proxy, it goes to your internet provider's resolver. Your provider then sees every site you visit, and sites that compare your resolver with your IP can tell you're using a VPN and where you actually are."],["How does this test find my resolver?","The page looks up a random name under edns.ip-api.com that has never been asked for, so no cache can answer. Your resolver has to ask ip-api's name server, which reports the resolver's address and network, plus your client subnet if the resolver passes it along (ECS). The test then compares the resolver's country with your IP's."],["How do I fix a DNS leak?","Clash / Mihomo: turn on its DNS with enhanced-mode fake-ip, or use TUN mode so every lookup goes through it. V2Ray / Xray clients: route DNS through the proxy (or set a DoH server that goes through it). In the browser, Secure DNS (DoH) also keeps lookups away from your provider. Then run this test again."],["My resolver is Cloudflare or Google in another city. Is that a leak?","Usually not. Public resolvers like 1.1.1.1 and 8.8.8.8 answer from the data center nearest the request, so the city can differ from your VPN exit. What gives you away is a resolver run by your home internet provider, or one in your home country while your IP is abroad."]],"webrtc-leak":[["What is a WebRTC leak?","To set up a call, WebRTC collects every address you could be reached at: the local ones on your network card, and the public one a STUN server sees. A page can read them with a few lines of script, no permission needed. If your VPN or proxy carries only the page's web requests, the STUN request goes out over your own connection, and the page learns your real IP behind the VPN."],["How does this test work?","The page opens a WebRTC connection to Cloudflare's and Google's STUN servers and lists the addresses they report (\"server reflexive\" candidates). It then compares them with the addresses this site, your other exits and the IPv6 test see. An address none of them show is a leak. The local addresses come from the \"host\" candidates. Nothing is stored or sent anywhere."],["How do I fix a WebRTC leak?","Proxy clients (Clash, V2Ray and others): turn on TUN mode so UDP goes through the proxy too. Chrome and Edge: the WebRTC Network Limiter extension, or uBlock Origin's \"Prevent WebRTC from leaking local IP addresses\". Brave: Settings → Privacy → WebRTC IP handling policy → Disable non-proxied UDP. Firefox: in about:config, set media.peerconnection.ice.default_address_only to true, or media.peerconnection.enabled to false to turn WebRTC off. Then run this test again."],["Is a local address or a .local name a leak?","A name ending in .local means the browser is hiding your local address (mDNS), which is what you want. A real local address such as 192.168.1.5 doesn't reveal where you are, but it makes your fingerprint more unique. An IPv6 address that differs from your IPv4 exit is a leak only if it isn't your IPv6 exit; the test checks it against the IPv6 address sites see."]],"browser-check":[["What does this check look at?","Whether the properties sites read (user agent, platform, languages, screen) were overwritten instead of coming from the browser; whether the drawing and audio functions used for fingerprints were replaced; whether the JavaScript engine matches the user agent; whether the language in the request headers matches the one in JavaScript; and whether Date and Intl agree on the time zone. Every other check on the page counts toward the grade too."],["Why do extensions that change the user agent get caught?","They change what JavaScript reads, but not the headers the browser sends with each request, the engine underneath, or the functions they wrap. Each mismatch is something a site can compare. To seem to be somewhere else, change the system itself: its language and time zone."],["Is anything stored?","Nothing that identifies you. The checks run in your browser, and the server only answers with what it can already see in this request, keeping no log. Afterwards the page adds one to anonymous counters (which values you had, how each check came out), with no IP and no record of a single visit. \"Don't count me\" on the page turns this off, and browsers that send Global Privacy Control are never counted."]],"timezone-check":[["How do sites know my time zone?","Through JavaScript's Intl and Date, which any page can use without a permission prompt. The IP's time zone comes from the same kind of location database that places your IP in a city."],["How do I fix a mismatch?","Set the system time zone to one in the country your IP is in: on Windows in Settings → Time & language, on macOS in System Settings → General → Date & Time, on phones in the date and time settings. An extension that only changes JavaScript's time zone is easy to catch; the browser environment check shows why."],["Why is a different zone in the same country only a warning?","Large countries have several time zones, and an IP is often placed in the right country but the wrong city. A zone in another country is a clear contradiction; a zone in the same country is only a hint."]],"proxy-check":[["How does the latency check work?","The page asks this site's nearest Cloudflare server for a tiny response a few times and keeps the fastest, timed from when the request leaves to when the first byte comes back. Cloudflare reports the round trip it measured on the TCP connection it served you on. Without a proxy the two are close; through one, Cloudflare only sees the proxy's part."],["Can a residential IP still be caught?","Yes. A residential IP passes the data center check, but the extra round trip, a DNS resolver in another country, a time zone that doesn't match and WebRTC addresses can all still give it away."],["Why does the check sometimes say \"couldn't read\"?","Over HTTP/3 there's no TCP connection, so Cloudflare has no round trip to report. Some networks also block the measuring requests."]],"language-check":[["Where do sites get my language from?","From the Accept-Language header, a line your browser sends with every request; from navigator.languages in JavaScript; and indirectly from your locale, keyboard layout, speech voices and the fonts your system has installed."],["How do I change it?","In the browser's language settings, put the language you want first. Changing it in only one place, such as with an extension that edits navigator.languages, leaves the header as it was, and the two then disagree."],["Why is English only marked as \"info\"?","English is used everywhere, so it says little about where you are. Other languages point to a country, and a mismatch with the IP is a hint."]],"canvas-fingerprint":[["What is a canvas fingerprint?","A page draws text and shapes on an invisible canvas, reads the pixels back and turns them into a short fixed code. The code is the same every time on your machine and different on other machines, so it works like an ID."],["How do I stop it?","Use a browser that adds random noise to canvas, WebGL and audio, like Brave (on by default) or Firefox with resistFingerprinting. The \"Fingerprint protection\" row shows whether two drawings came out different."],["Do privacy extensions help?","Some do, by wrapping the drawing functions. But that can itself be noticed: the browser environment check flags replaced functions."]],"user-agent":[["What is a user agent?","A line of text your browser sends with every request, naming the browser, its version and your system. Chromium browsers also send client hints: a newer, more structured version of the same information."],["Why does it say Windows 10 when I'm on Windows 11?","For privacy, browsers froze the user agent at Windows 10 (and macOS at 10.15). The real version comes from client hints, and that's what this page shows."],["Can I fake it?","You can change the string, but the JavaScript engine, the graphics card and client hints usually tell a different story. The checks on this page show where."]],"extensions":[["How can a site see my extensions?","It can't list them, but it can see what they leave behind: objects like window.ethereum, attributes on the page, elements they insert, scripts they add. Extensions that leave nothing behind stay invisible."],["Why does it matter?","Extensions narrow you down. A crypto wallet, developer tools or a particular translator says something about who you are, and makes your fingerprint more unique."],["What does the tampering check have to do with it?","Extensions that change your user agent, language or time zone, or wrap the drawing functions used for fingerprints, rewrite what sites read. That's what the check at the top looks for."]],"anti-detect-browser":[["What gives an anti-detect browser away?","Parts that don't agree. A user agent that says Chrome 156 while the engine lacks features Chrome shipped long ago; a Worker that reads a different user agent or language than the page; navigator.platform saying Mac with a Windows user agent; WebGL naming one maker as vendor and another as the card. Sites rarely look for the browser itself; they look for these contradictions."],["Why does the engine's real version matter?","The user agent is just text, and any browser can rewrite it. The JavaScript features an engine supports can't be rewritten the same way: each Chromium version adds functions (Promise.try in 128, RegExp.escape in 136), so which ones exist pins the engine to a range of versions. A user agent outside that range belongs to a profile, not a real browser."],["How do I make a profile pass?","Pick a user agent version that matches the browser's core; prefer changes made in the browser's core over injected scripts, so a Worker reads the same values; choose system, platform and graphics vendor as one set; and set the time zone and language to the proxy's country. Then check again here."]],"icloud-private-relay":[["Can sites tell I use Private Relay?","Yes. Apple publishes the list of its exit addresses, and this page checks your address against it. A site sees a relay exit, not your own address, and usually a city or region near you, which Apple keeps on purpose so local content still works."],["Is Private Relay a VPN?","No. It covers Safari and unencrypted traffic from apps, not every app; you can't point it at another country; and it hides your address from sites, and the sites you visit from your network provider. To a site it looks like a privacy relay, not like someone abroad."],["Why does WebRTC show another relay address?","Each connection gets its own exit, so the page and WebRTC can come out of different relay addresses. If both are relay exits, nothing is leaking; the check only warns when a real address shows up next to them."]],"warp-check":[["How can a site tell I use WARP?","Sites behind Cloudflare can read Cloudflare's trace, which says warp=on for traffic that comes through WARP. Even elsewhere, a WARP address belongs to Cloudflare's own network (AS13335), which sites treat as a data center rather than a home connection."],["Does WARP hide where I am?","Mostly not. WARP exits near you, usually in your own country, so a site can still place you roughly. It's built for privacy and speed, not for appearing somewhere else. Sites see a Cloudflare address instead of your internet provider's."],["What does warp=plus mean?","The trace says plus for WARP+, the paid tier that uses Cloudflare's faster routes. To a site the two look the same: a Cloudflare address with WARP on."]],"http3-ech":[["Why isn't HTTP/3 used?","HTTP/3 runs over UDP port 443. A browser learns that a site supports it from a first answer over TCP, so it may not switch on a first visit. But if UDP can't get out at all, a network or proxy is blocking it or doesn't carry UDP. Many proxy setups carry only TCP."],["What is ECH, and why is it off?","Encrypted Client Hello encrypts the name of the site you connect to, which TLS otherwise sends in the clear for anyone along the way to read. The browser needs the site's keys from a DNS HTTPS record: Firefox reads them only over its own DNS over HTTPS, Chrome needs a resolver that returns HTTPS records, and Safari doesn't use ECH yet."],["What does a classical key exchange mean?","Chrome 124+, Firefox 132+ and Safari 26 offer X25519MLKEM768 by default. A current browser that ends up with plain X25519 points to something in between making its own TLS connection: an antivirus, a company proxy, or a proxy client that terminates TLS. On older browsers it just means the browser is old."]],"ai-agent":[["Can a website tell an AI agent from me?","Often, yes. AI browsing agents are handed the same Chrome you use, so the fingerprint looks ordinary. But the tools that drive them (Playwright, Puppeteer, Selenium, CDP) leave traces: missing browser objects, injected globals, a window with no frame, and clicks that arrive with no mouse path before them. Research on browser agents (FP-Agent, arXiv 2605.01247) found behaviour separates them better than the fingerprint."],["What is a headless browser?","A browser with no visible window, used by servers, scripts and agent tools. It usually misses window.chrome, reports a zero-size window, has no languages, or says HeadlessChrome in its user agent. A site reads all that without a permission prompt."],["Does my browser look automated if I use an extension?","Only if the extension injects driver-like globals into the page. A normal privacy extension changes what JavaScript reads, not the browser's window, and lie detection covers that. The row on this page names what was found, so you can tell."]],"client-hints":[["What are Client Hints?","A standard for sending parts of your browser's identity as HTTP headers, requested by the site with Accept-CH. The low-entropy ones (brand, version, platform) go with every request; the high-entropy ones (architecture, bitness, model, platform version, form factors, 32-bit mode) are only sent once a site asks. The page has the Worker request them, then reads them back on the next request."],["Why compare them with navigator?","A user-agent switcher or an anti-detect browser can rewrite what JavaScript reads, but it cannot change the headers the browser itself sends. If navigator says one thing and the headers say another, the page flags it. That is stronger evidence than either value alone."],["Does my browser support Client Hints?","Chromium browsers (Chrome, Edge, Opera, Brave, Samsung Internet) do. Firefox and Safari don't send them yet, and the row says so instead of guessing. A VPN or proxy does not change them; they are about the browser, not the network."]],"stats":[["Where do these numbers come from?","Each time someone finishes a check here, the page adds one to anonymous counters: how each check came out, and readings like the system, browser and screen. No IP and no record of a single visit are kept, so there are only totals here."],["Why do so many people get caught?","Many who test here already use a proxy or VPN and want to see how well they hide. That's why split routing, mismatched clocks and DNS leaks are far more common here than among internet users at large."],["How often is it updated?","The page reads the latest numbers each time it opens; the totals are worked out once a minute. \"Commonest\" lists only values shared by 20 checks or more, so rare values never show up here."]],"vpn-leak":[["What does a VPN leak test check?","The places a VPN usually leaks: which country's resolver looked the page's name up (a different country from the IP suggests the query left the tunnel), the addresses WebRTC reports, whether different kinds of site see different exits, whether IPv6 bypasses the tunnel, and whether the address is a public Tor exit. Each is reported separately, never merged into one verdict."],["Does a DNS leak mean my VPN is broken?","Not necessarily. With Secure DNS (DoH) the browser can send its own queries, outside the VPN; split routing, a fake-ip resolver and a company gateway do the same. Compare the DNS row's country with the IP's, then read the connection fingerprint: together they say whether the query really bypassed the tunnel."],["Why do different sites see different exits?","The usual cause is rule-based splitting: one proxy client sends local sites direct and foreign sites through the proxy. A mobile carrier, a company network or security software can split traffic the same way. So it is a measurement, not proof that you configured a proxy."],["How do I stop WebRTC leaks?","Put the proxy client in TUN mode, or route WebRTC through it; in the browser you can limit WebRTC (Chrome's WebRTC Network Limiter). Turning WebRTC off stops the leak but only hides the reading: it does not prove the rest of the traffic is in the tunnel."]],"webgpu":[["What is WebGPU?","A newer graphics API for the web, closer to modern cards than WebGL: compute shaders, finer control of memory and queues. A site asks navigator.gpu for an adapter and gets its vendor, architecture, description, limits and feature list."],["What does WebGPU add to a fingerprint?","The adapter name and architecture point at a card's model or family, and the limit and feature lists follow the driver and browser version. They stay stable on one machine, and together with canvas, WebGL and fonts they make a fingerprint more specific."],["Does my browser support it?","Chrome and Edge 113+, Firefox 141+ on Windows, Safari 26+; it needs HTTPS. A browser without it says so in the row instead of leaving a blank."],["Why does WebGPU name a different card than WebGL?","Laptops often have two: pages draw on the integrated card by default, and WebGPU asking for a high-performance adapter may get the discrete one. The page reads both the high-performance and low-power adapters and lists what each reports; when only one exists, they are the same card."]]};
